<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">First Mexican Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1823 CE – 1835 CE · capital Mexico City</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mexico's first federal republic, created by the Constitution of 1824 and torn by coups between liberals, conservatives and Santa Anna.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a>, a constituent congress adopted the federal Constitution of 4 October 1824, modelled partly on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> and the Spanish constitution of 1812. It created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-federal-republic-of-mexico">United Mexican States</a>, with nineteen states and four territories, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalupe-victoria">Guadalupe Victoria</a>, a hero of the independence war, became the first president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> remained the sole permitted religion, and the army and church kept their legal privileges. Politics was dominated by rival Masonic lodges and military revolts: only <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-victoria">Victoria</a> completed his term. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicente-guerrero">Vicente Guerrero</a> abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1829, the year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Santa Anna</a> defeated a Spanish reconquest attempt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tampico">Tampico</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentin-gomez-farias">Valentín Gómez Farías</a> launched anticlerical reforms in 1833, and the treasury depended on customs revenue and foreign loans.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a> turned against the reformers in 1834, and in 1835 a conservative congress replaced the federal system with centralist laws, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siete-leyes">Siete Leyes</a>, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/centralist-republic-of-mexico">Centralist Republic of Mexico</a>. The change helped provoke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas-revolution">Texas Revolution</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/first-mexican-republic.jpg" alt="First Mexican Republic" width="320" height="183"><figcaption>Image: Ludovicus Ferdinandus can have elements by Sodacan and Heralder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandera_de_la_Primera_Rep%C3%BAblica_Federal_de_los_Estados_Unidos_Mexicanos.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1823 CE – 1835 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mexico City</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Guadalupe Victoria, Vicente Guerrero, Antonio López de Santa Anna</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>, <a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>United Mexican States (1824-1835), First Federal Republic of Mexico</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1829&amp;focus=first-mexican-republic" aria-label="First Mexican Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2001 1461 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where First Mexican Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2321 1717L2321 1717M2321 1717L2321 1717"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2321 1717h0M2321 1717h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2321 1717h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2321 1717h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mexico City. <a href="/atlas?year=1829&amp;focus=first-mexican-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
